. For example, if the user has B5 and B8, maybe there's a B6 or B7 to form a chow. But the unshown B6 has 1 left, B7 has 2. So if the user has B5 and B8, maybe they can form B5-B6-B7 or B6-B7-B8, but they don't have B6 or B7. So maybe B5 or B8 aren't useful for chows.\n\nLooking at T3. The user has T3 in hand. The meld is [T6, T7, T8]. So T3 could be part of a T1-T2-T3 chow if they have T1 and T2. But the user doesn't have T1 or T2. The unshown T1 has 1 left, T2 has 0. So T2 is already gone. So T3 can't form a chow. So T3 is a lone tile. If they discard T3, someone might take it for a chi. For example, if someone has T1 and T2, but T2 is already gone. So maybe T3 is safe to discard.\n\nLooking at W2. The user has W2. The unshown W3 is 0, so W3 is already used up. So W2 can't form a chi with W1 and W3, since W3 is gone. But the user has W1 and W2. If they have W3, they could form a chow. But W3 is not in their hand. So W2 is a lone tile. Discarding W2 might be safe.\n\nW5 is in the hand. The user has W5 and W4. They also have W7 in the meld. So W4 and W5 could be part of a W4-W5-W6 chow if they have W6. But the unshown W6 has 2 left. So maybe if they have W6, they can form that. But they don't have W6. So W5 is a lone tile.\n\nW1 is in the hand. They have W1 and W2. If they had W3, they could form a chow. But W3 is gone. So W1 is a lone tile.\n\nB5 is in the hand. The user has B5. The unshown B4 is 0, B6 is 1, B7 is 2. So if they have B5, maybe they can form B4-B5-B6 or B5-B6-B7. But B4 is gone, so only B5-B6-B7 is possible. They don't have B6 or B7. So B5 is a lone tile.\n\nB8 is in the hand. The user has B8. The unshown B7 is 2, B9 is 3. So if they have B7 and B9, they could form B7-B8-B9. But they don't have B7 or B9. So B8 is a lone tile.\n\nNow, considering which tile to discard. The goal is to maximize the chance of forming a winning hand while minimizing the risk of giving others a chance to win.\n\nLooking at the hand: The user has W1, W2, W4, W5, W8; T3; B5,