s risky to discard it. Similarly for J3.\n\nSo between the options, playing B8 or B9 might be better. Let's check the unshown tiles: B8 has 3 left, B9 has 2. So B8 is more available. If the user plays B8, others might take it, but if not, they can still get B7 for a chow. Alternatively, playing B9 might be less safe if someone needs it.\n\nAnother thought: the user has T5. If they play T5, it's a middle tile. If they keep it, maybe they can form a chow with T4 and T6, but T6 is already out. So T5 is just a single tile. Playing it might be safe.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the user can play T5, B8, W2, W8, W4, J2, J3, B9.\n\nIf I play B8, the hand becomes [W2, W4, W8, T5, B9, J2, J3]. Then the user has B9 left. If they can get B7, they can form B7, B8, B9 (but B8 is already played). Wait, no, if they play B8, then B8 is out. So they can't form that chow. Wait, no, if they play B8, they are discarding it. So the user would have B9 in hand, but B8 is no longer in hand. So the chow would need B7 and B8. But B8 is discarded, so if someone else takes it, the user can't use it. So maybe playing B8 is not ideal if they want to form a B7, B8, B9 chow.\n\nWait, the user's original hand has B8 and B9. If they play B8, they lose the chance to form a B7, B8, B9 chow. But if they play B9, they can still keep B8 and get B7. So maybe playing B9 is better for that.\n\nSo between B8 and B9, playing B9 allows keeping B8 to form a chow with B7. So playing B9 might be better.\n\nAlternatively, if the user plays B8, they can keep B9 and maybe form a chow with B7 and B8 (but B8 is discarded). So that's not possible. Therefore, playing B9 is better for the B7-B8-B9 chow.\n\nTherefore, playing B9 might be the better option. Let me check the unshown tiles: B9 has 2 left. So there's a chance someone else has it. But if the user keeps B8, they can get B7 to form a chow.\n\nSo the best move might be to play B9, keeping B8 in hand to form a chow with B7 if available.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation